description:
Location: `crate-jmap-server/src/backend.rs:275-355`

  pub enum BackendSetError<E> {
      SetError(SetError),
      Other(E),
  }

  impl<E: std::fmt::Display> std::fmt::Display for BackendSetError<E> {
      fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {
          match self {
              Self::SetError(se) => write!(f, "set error: {se}"),
              Self::Other(e) => write!(f, "{e}"),       // <-- arbitrary backend string passes through
          }
      }
  }

CONCERN
`BackendSetError::Other(E)` (and the parallel `BackendChangesError::Other(E)` at line 339) wraps a backend's associated `Self::Error` type. The Display impl writes whatever Display the backend's error chose, unredacted.

Every reference `MemoryBackend` constructs `MemoryError(format!("..."))` strings that include various input values:
- `crate-jmap-mail-server/src/memory.rs:1172` — `format!("blob not found: {blob_id}")`
- `crate-jmap-mail-server/src/memory.rs:384` — `format!("invalid state token: {since_state}")`
- Many other sites across the 7 sibling memory backends (per .82 sweep).

For the reference memory backends today, the interpolated values (Ids, state tokens) are not credential-grade — so today's exposure is small. The CONCERN is contract-level:

The `MailBackend::Error` / `ChatBackend::Error` / etc. trait associated types have NO documented contract saying 'do not put credential material into your Display impl'. A backend author who has not read every other crate's security model could plausibly:
- Include a sieve-script fragment in an error: `format!("sieve parse error at line 42: {script_excerpt}")` — leaks the script body.
- Include an `Authorization` header value when reporting a downstream-service failure.
- Include an email body fragment when an email-size-quota error is constructed.

Each of those errors then propagates through `BackendSetError::Other` → `RequestError`'s Display (response.rs:91-108) → operator logs once tracing is wired up. The library offers no guardrail.

WHAT GOES WRONG
Confidentiality, conditionally. Today's reference backends don't trip this; the risk is a downstream backend author who follows the trait contract without reading the implicit security model. Defense-in-depth gap.

PROPOSED FIX
Add a documented requirement on the `Error` associated type of every Backend trait:

  /// The associated error type for storage-layer failures.
  ///
  /// # Security
  ///
  /// The `Display` impl of this type MUST NOT include any of the following
  /// in its output:
  /// - credential material (auth tokens, passwords, push verification codes,
  ///   invite codes)
  /// - blob content (email bodies, sieve scripts, file contents)
  /// - any value derived from `Authorization`-header bytes
  ///
  /// Errors that wrap downstream-service failures should sanitize the
  /// downstream error text before constructing the Display string.
  type Error: std::error::Error + Send + Sync + 'static;

Apply to MailBackend, ChatBackend, CalendarsBackend, TasksBackend, ContactsBackend, FileNodeBackend, SharingBackend, JmapBackend supertrait, and SieveBackend. Per the canonical-templates rule, this is one of those workspace-wide doc sweeps.

Optionally add a regression test for each reference MemoryBackend that constructs every MemoryError(...) variant and asserts none of the resulting strings contain literal canary substrings — that's the auth.rs:443-475 pattern applied at the backend layer.

ORACLE
- Closed JMAP-sc1b.79 (BearerAuth/BasicAuth Debug redaction) — same class of finding applied at the Error-trait level rather than the type-derive level.
- AGENTS.md 'Canonical Templates' rule — Backend traits are the cookie-cutter shape that every backend implementor follows; the security contract belongs in the trait doc.

RISK CLASSIFICATION
P3. Defense-in-depth contract gap. Today's code does not exploit this; the future maintainer who writes a downstream backend can.
